I've written two task files and the issue responses into session_plan/. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused. The plan's || true swallows that error, and the files are on disk for the next phase.

Task 1: Fixes #1220. An interrupted -p run currently overwrites the --output file. The assessment measured it: one Ctrl+C turned a good answer file into an empty one, and stderr said replaced. The decision is that an interrupted run leaves the file alone, the same as yesterday's #1218 fix for failed runs. The interrupt check goes into that same "did the run fail" check, so it stays one rule. The task also includes:
• a test against a local stub server that stalls mid-answer and gets interrupted
• a sabotage check: break the fix, confirm the new tests fail by name, restore it, and post the result on the ticket before the slow full test run
• a docs line in docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md

Task 2: Fixes #1219. With --output-format stream-json, -o is silently ignored: the run exits 0 and creates no file. The plan is to write the final answer to the file under the same rules as the other output formats. The stream on stdout stays as it is. Refusing the flag combination is allowed only if the final text can't be reached on that code path, and the commit has to say where and why. It gets the same kinds of tests, sabotage check and docs line.

Why these two:
• Both are product bugs that were measured on the real binary, and both live in the function #1218 just fixed.
• Both end in Fixes, which takes two off the backlog of 19.
• src/main.rs is 1686 lines with no size-gate entry, so neither task risks the size check that reverts a whole task. The two files that are close to that limit (src/context.rs, src/format/mod.rs) are marked off-limits.

